window.KB_EXPLAIN_EN_ADD({"L7-7.1.1":"Coding-from-scratch questions don't assess \"did you finish it\" but the **process**. A fixed answering process lets the interviewer see your engineering judgment at every step, in both Chinese- and English-language interviews.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints (about 30 seconds): tensor shapes, batch dimension, whether masking is needed, whether numpy / torch is allowed, whether it must be differentiable.\n2. Write the function signature and 2–3 tests first (about 1 minute), e.g., for attention, \"upper-triangular weights are 0 when causal\" and \"rows sum to 1\".\n3. Narrate shapes as you write, putting `(B, H, T, d)` comments at the end of lines.\n4. Self-check when done: numerical stability (subtract the max), edge cases (empty input, k > N, T=0), and whether in-place operations corrupt the input.\n5. Proactively state the complexity and bottlenecks, then describe how a production version would differ: attention → FlashAttention, top-k → argpartition, list → vector index.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWriting tests first immediately sets you apart from candidates who \"finish but don't know whether it's right\"; in interviews that allow AI assistance, the ability to verify is an even more central scoring point. The cost is an extra minute or two up front, but it significantly reduces the risk of rework later.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The two most common follow-ups: \"why did you do it this way\" and \"what if X changes\".\n- When practicing, cover all five stages for every problem: \"clarify → test → implement → complexity → productionize\".","L7-7.1.2":"Some behaviors are close to an automatic fail, because they expose gaps in fundamentals or engineering awareness rather than a momentary slip.\n\n**The common list**\n- **Coding without talking**: the interviewer can't assess your thinking, and you have no way to recover when something goes wrong.\n- **Writing softmax as `exp/sum` without subtracting the max**: with large logits, exp overflows and produces nan.\n- **Storing k before rotation in the KV cache**: every step has to re-rotate all of history, which is slow and prone to misalignment.\n- **Beam search without a finished pool**: sequences that hit EOS keep occupying beam slots, and comparisons between long and short sequences go wrong.\n- **Letting tool-call exceptions bubble up**: a single bad argument crashes the whole agent, instead of being fed back to the model as an observation to correct.\n- **Using `time.sleep` in asyncio**: it blocks the event loop and stalls every coroutine; use `await asyncio.sleep` instead.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe first three are numerical and correctness problems on the model side; the last three are robustness problems on the agent / systems side. Candidates with a research background are more likely to slip on the latter, which is exactly where AI engineering roles separate candidates.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use this list as a self-check after writing code, going through it item by item before handing in.\n- The planted bugs in find-the-bug rounds are often variants of these points.","L7-7.1.3":"Softmax and cross-entropy are the most basic coding-from-scratch questions, testing numerical stability, gradient derivation, and engineering details such as `ignore_index` and label smoothing.\n\n**How it works**\n- Softmax is shift-invariant: `softmax(x) = softmax(x - max x)`. Subtract each row's max first, so the largest exponent is `exp(0)=1` and nothing overflows.\n- Compute log-softmax as `z_y - logsumexp(z)`; don't write `log(softmax)`, to avoid `log(0)`.\n- CE = `-1/N · Σ log p_{n,y_n}`, averaged only over valid positions; in SFT, set prompt positions to -100 so only the response contributes to the loss.\n- Gradient: `∂L/∂z = (p - onehot(y)) / N`. The derivative of logsumexp is softmax, and after the cross terms cancel the result is very clean, which is also why the two are always fused into a single op.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nComplexity is O(N·V). V is the vocabulary size (128k for Llama 3), so lm_head + CE is a major memory consumer, and Liger-Kernel / chunked CE compute the logits in chunks. Common bugs: dividing by zero when `mask.sum()==0`; in numpy, -100 is treated as a negative index and silently fetches the 100th-from-last element.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: `logits=[1000,1000,0]` produces no nan, and the analytic gradient matches the numerical gradient.\n- Label smoothing is almost never used in LLM pretraining: it lowers confidence in the correct token and hurts calibration.\n- In distillation, logits are divided by T, and the KD loss is multiplied by T² to compensate for the gradient scaling.","L7-7.1.4":"Scaled dot-product attention is the core operator of the Transformer, testing shape manipulation, the scaling factor, the two representations of masks, and semantic alignment with PyTorch SDPA.\n\n**How it works**\n- `q (B,H,L,d) · kᵀ (B,H,d,S) → scores (B,H,L,S)`, multiply by `1/√d`, add the mask (-inf), softmax over the last dimension, then multiply by `v (B,H,S,dv)`.\n- Dividing by √d: if the components of q and k are independent with variance 1, the dot product has variance d; without scaling, softmax saturates toward one-hot and gradients vanish.\n- The mask is applied before softmax; for a bool mask, be explicit about whether True means keep or mask out. For a full forward pass with L=S, a lower-triangular mask suffices; for incremental decoding with a KV cache, L<S, and you must use `tril(diagonal=S-L)` for bottom-right alignment.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTime is O(B·H·L·S·d), memory O(B·H·L·S). FlashAttention uses tiling plus online softmax to cut memory to O(L), but FLOPs are unchanged; it optimizes IO. Common bugs: scaling by d_model instead of head_dim; filling the mask with 0 instead of -inf; NaN when an entire row is masked.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: upper-triangular weights are 0, rows sum to 1, and results match a naive four-nested-loop implementation.\n- Online softmax: maintain `m, l, acc`, and rescale by `exp(m_old - m_new)` when a new block arrives.\n- When padding and causal masks coexist, combine the two bool masks with `&`.","L7-7.1.5":"This is a classic trap in PyTorch's `F.scaled_dot_product_attention`: the causal mask generated by `is_causal=True` is **top-left aligned**, so during incremental decoding with a KV cache it wrongly masks out history.\n\n**How it works**\n- In the SDPA reference implementation, `is_causal=True` corresponds to `torch.ones(L, S).tril(diagonal=0)`, i.e., the i-th query can only see the first i+1 keys.\n- This is correct for a full forward pass with L == S; but in incremental decoding L < S (the new token should see all of history), and top-left alignment lets a single new token see only the first key, masking out all history except the first key.\n- The right approach: use `is_causal` only when L == S; otherwise pass an explicit bottom-right-aligned mask `torch.ones(T, S).tril(diagonal=S - T)`; when L == 1, no mask is needed at all.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis bug raises no error; it just silently degrades generation quality and is hard to spot from the output. The only reliable way to catch it is a consistency assertion that \"token-by-token incremental decoding == full forward\". It also shows that you must read a library API's semantics carefully before calling it, rather than relying on intuition.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how do you apply the attention mask with a cache\", proactively point out the difference between bottom-right alignment and `is_causal`.\n- Likewise, don't pass `is_causal=True` directly for incremental decoding with cross-attention.","L7-7.1.6":"Multi-head attention splits D dimensions into H heads computed in parallel; GQA (grouped-query attention) lets multiple query heads share one set of K/V heads to shrink the KV cache.\n\n**How it works**\n- MHA: `x (B,T,D) → linear projection → view(B,T,H,d) → transpose(1,2) → (B,H,T,d)`; after attention, transpose back to `(B,T,H,d)`, `reshape(B,T,D)`, then apply the output projection Wo. Calling `view` directly after transpose errors because memory is non-contiguous; use `reshape` or call `.contiguous()` first.\n- Parameter count: MHA's four projections total `4D²` (excluding bias); in GQA the K and V projections are only `D × (H_kv·d)`.\n- Before computing GQA, use `repeat_interleave(H/H_kv)` to replicate K/V heads to H; in torch 2.5+, `enable_gqa=True` does this for you; MQA is the special case `H_kv=1`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nKV cache size is proportional to the number of kv heads. Llama 3 70B uses 8 kv heads with 64 query heads, shrinking the cache 8x with quality close to MHA. Replication happens only at compute time; the cache still stores the unreplicated K/V, otherwise the memory saving is lost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertion: with `H_kv = H`, GQA matches a per-head MHA reference implementation exactly.\n- MLA (DeepSeek) caches a low-rank compressed latent vector, and the RoPE part needs a separate decoupled dimension.\n- When writing reshapes, put a shape comment at the end of every line; this is where interviewers most easily judge whether you really understand how heads are split and merged.","L7-7.1.7":"Positional encodings inject order information into attention. Sinusoidal is an absolute positional encoding added to the embeddings; RoPE rotates q and k so that their inner product depends only on relative position, and is the standard in modern LLMs.\n\n**How it works**\n- Sinusoidal: `PE[pos,2i] = sin(pos/10000^{2i/d})`, `PE[pos,2i+1] = cos(...)`.\n- RoPE: pair up head_dim dimensions as complex numbers and multiply position m by `e^{i·m·θ_j}`, `θ_j = base^{-2j/d}`. The HF / Llama rotate-half formulation is `x·cos + rotate_half(x)·sin`, with `rotate_half([x1,x2]) = [-x2, x1]`.\n- Rotation is an orthogonal transform: `<R_m q, R_n k> = <q, R_{n-m} k>`, so the inner product depends only on m−n.\n- The KV cache stores k **after rotation**: historical tokens' positions are fixed, so they need rotating only once; q is rotated at each step according to the current position.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRotate-half (pairing the first and second halves) and interleaved (pairing adjacent dimensions, the original paper / GPT-J style) are mathematically equivalent but use different weight layouts; mixing them fails silently. Other common bugs: rotating q but not k; restarting positions from 0 instead of `start_pos` during incremental decoding.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assert `score(3,1) == score(10,8)` and that rotation preserves norm.\n- Long-context extrapolation: PI, NTK-aware, YaRN; Llama 3 raised the base to 500k. A larger base makes long distances more distinguishable but lowers short-range resolution.","L7-7.1.8":"Normalization layers and the FFN are the other two components of a Transformer block. The default combination in modern LLMs (Llama / Qwen / DeepSeek) is RMSNorm + pre-norm + SwiGLU.\n\n**How it works**\n- LayerNorm: `(x-μ)/√(σ²+ε)·γ + β`, normalizing over the last (feature) dimension, with eps inside the square root.\n- RMSNorm: `x/√(mean(x²)+ε)·γ`, dropping mean subtraction and β, saving one reduction, roughly 10%–30% faster (implementation-dependent). It equals LN when the input has zero mean.\n- SwiGLU FFN: `down(silu(gate(x)) * up(x))`, three matrices. To match the parameter count of a two-matrix FFN with 4d hidden size, the hidden size is `8/3·d` rounded up to a multiple of 256 (Llama 2 7B: 4096 → 11008).\n- Under mixed precision, upcast to fp32 to compute `mean(x²)`, then cast back to the original dtype.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPre-norm (`x + f(norm(x))`) keeps the residual path an identity mapping, making deep training more stable and less dependent on long warmup; post-norm sometimes ends up slightly better but is hard to train. The multiplicative interaction of gating is more expressive, and PaLM's ablations show SwiGLU is better at the same parameter count.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The motivation for RMSNorm: LN's benefit comes mainly from re-scaling, not re-centering.\n- QK-norm: apply RMSNorm to q and k over head_dim to prevent attention logits from blowing up (adopted by Qwen3 and OLMo 2).\n- Norm γ parameters usually get no weight decay.","L7-7.1.9":"Assembling attention, RoPE, RMSNorm, and SwiGLU into a mini GPT and then adding KV-cache incremental decoding is the capstone coding-from-scratch problem on the model side. The core question is **how to prove the cache implementation is correct**.\n\n**How it works**\n- Block: `x = x + Attn(RMSNorm(x)); x = x + FFN(RMSNorm(x))` (pre-norm).\n- Output: `logits = RMSNorm(x) @ tok_emb.T`, i.e., weight tying, with lm_head sharing weights with the embedding.\n- Generation: first prefill the prompt to get the cache, then feed only 1 token per step, with `start_pos` = current length and cos/sin sliced by `start_pos`.\n- Correctness assertion: the logits from token-by-token incremental forward passes match a single full forward pass position by position (atol 1e-6), and \"prefill half + increment the other half\" also matches.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWithout a cache, generating N tokens is O(N²·L); with a cache it is O(N·L), at the cost of memory. A `torch.cat` at every step causes O(S²) copying, so production systems preallocate or page (PagedAttention). When results don't match, bisect layer by layer comparing q/k/v and post-RoPE k; the most common causes are positions starting from 0, misaligned masks, and caching unrotated k.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Under a 45-minute limit: get the full forward pass passing tests first, and add the cache last.\n- The cache doesn't store q: past q's never participate in any further computation.\n- The generation function should use `no_grad` / `inference_mode`.","L7-7.1.10":"Estimating KV cache memory is the most common \"do the math on the spot\" question in inference interviews, used to judge how long a context and how much concurrency you can support.\n\n**How it works**\n- Formula: `2 × n_layer × n_kv_head × head_dim × S × bytes`. The 2 is for K and V, S is sequence length, and bytes is bytes per element (2 for bf16).\n- Llama 3 8B (32 layers, 8 kv heads, hd 128, bf16): per token = 2·32·8·128·2 B = 128 KB, so an 8k context is about 1 GB.\n- A 7B model with 32 layers, 8 KV heads, and hd 128 needs about 4.3 GB for a single 32k-context request; with 32-head MHA it would be 4x, about 17 GB.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe formula is proportional to the number of kv heads, which is exactly the motivation for GQA / MQA / MLA. The decode phase reads all weights and the KV cache at every step and is memory-bound, so a larger cache means more bandwidth pressure. When production GPU memory is maxed out, first compute `per-token cache × average context × concurrent sequences` and compare it with `GPU memory − weights − activation reserve`, and look at the long-tail distribution rather than the average.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Ways to cut the cache, from lowest to highest cost: limit `max_model_len` / concurrency, KV cache quantization, GQA/MLA models, prefix sharing, admission control.\n- Be able to say \"trade memory for compute\", and distinguish compute-bound prefill from memory-bound decode.","L7-7.1.11":"Cross-attention is a small modification of MHA, common in encoder-decoder and multimodal models; an MoE layer replaces one FFN with multiple experts plus routing. Both are frequent coding-from-scratch questions in real interview reports.\n\n**How it works**\n- Cross-attention: the Q projection comes from the target sequence `xq (B,T,D)`, the K and V projections from memory `xkv (B,S,D)`, giving a score matrix `(B,H,T,S)` where T and S can differ; with `xkv = xq` it is self-attention. Causal masks apply only to self-attention; cross-attention usually needs only a key-side padding mask, broadcast to `(B,1,1,S)`. Memory doesn't change, so its K/V need computing only once at prefill.\n- MoE: `probs = softmax(x @ Wg)`; each token takes its top-k experts, with gate weights renormalized within the top-k. Do batched matrix multiplies **grouped by expert**, not a double loop over tokens. The load-balancing loss (Switch style) is `E·Σ f_e·P_e`, equal to 1 at perfect balance; capacity is `ceil(cf·N·k/E)`, and overflow tokens are dropped, falling back on the residual.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWithout load balancing, routing collapses: a few experts take most tokens and the others get no gradient; with expert parallelism, the slowest GPU determines the step time. DeepSeek-V3's technical report says it uses an auxiliary-loss-free balancing strategy, adjusting with bias terms that affect only routing and don't enter the gradient.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Invariance test for cross-attention: changing memory that is masked out by padding leaves the output unchanged.\n- MoE tests: with `k=E` it equals all experts weighted by probability; when routing is skewed to a single expert, the auxiliary loss becomes noticeably larger.","L7-7.2.1":"Decoding-time sampling determines how the model picks the next token from the probability distribution. Temperature adjusts how peaked the distribution is, while top-k / top-p / min-p cut off the long tail, trading off quality against diversity.\n\n**How it works**\n- Temperature: `logits / T`; T<1 is sharper, T>1 flatter; as T→0 it approaches argmax, but T=0 must go to greedy separately rather than dividing by zero.\n- Top-k: use the k-th largest logit as the threshold and set the rest to -inf.\n- Top-p: accumulate probabilities in descending order and keep tokens up to and including the point where cumulative probability first reaches ≥ p; checking `cum - prob < p` naturally guarantees at least 1 token is kept.\n- Min-p: threshold = `p_min × max_prob`, adapting to the model's confidence; it was an ICLR 2025 oral and has landed in HF transformers and vLLM.\n- Common order: temperature → top-k → top-p → min-p; libraries may differ, so state your choice in the interview. All filtering sets -inf in logit space, followed by a final softmax and sampling.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTop-p needs a sort, O(V log V), so vLLM and others apply top-k first to shrink the sort before top-p. At high temperature, top-p lets long-tail noise in, whereas min-p anchors the cutoff on the max probability and is more stable. Common bugs: using `cum > p` and keeping nothing; zeroing in probability space instead of setting -inf in logit space.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: with `probs=[0.5,0.3,0.15,0.05]`, top-p=0.9 keeps 3 tokens, and top-p=0.4 still keeps 1.\n- Results can differ even with the same seed: changing batch composition changes the order of floating-point reductions (batch invariance).","L7-7.2.2":"Beam search keeps the `beam_size` prefixes with the highest cumulative log-prob at each step, getting closer to the globally optimal sequence than greedy, and is a common decoding method for NMT and ASR.\n\n**How it works**\n- Maintain `beam_size` (score, seq) pairs; extend each with candidate tokens, merge, and take the top `beam_size`.\n- Sequences that hit EOS move into the **finished pool** and are no longer extended; stop when max_len is reached or the best score in the finished pool can no longer be beaten.\n- Cumulative log-prob only decreases, favoring short sequences, so at the end normalize by length with `score / len^α` (GNMT style: `((5+len)/6)^α`).\n- Correctness tests: with beam width = |V|^L it equals exhaustive search; beam=1 equals greedy; beam=4's score ≥ greedy's.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nChat LLMs rarely use beam search: maximizing likelihood favors generic, repetitive high-frequency sentences (the likelihood trap), sampling reads more naturally, and beam width multiplies the KV cache. Complexity is O(L·beam·V). In serving, multiple beams with the same prefix can share the prefix's cache pages via copy-on-write, which vLLM's paging naturally supports.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Without a finished pool: finished candidates keep occupying slots or even get wrongly extended.\n- Constrained decoding (must output valid JSON) sets illegal tokens to -inf at each step and can be combined with greedy, sampling, or beam search.","L7-7.2.3":"Byte-level BPE is the tokenizer foundation for the GPT series, Llama 3, and other models: starting from UTF-8 bytes, it repeatedly merges the most frequent adjacent pair and learns an ordered merge table.\n\n**How it works**\n- The initial vocabulary is 256 bytes, and `ids` = the text's UTF-8 byte sequence.\n- Repeat `vocab_size - 256` times: count adjacent pair frequencies, take the most frequent pair, assign it a new id `256+i`, and replace that pair in the sequence with the new id; record `merges[pair] = idx` and `vocab[idx] = vocab[p0] + vocab[p1]`.\n- Decoding: concatenate the vocab bytes and UTF-8 decode, using `errors=\"replace\"` to handle partial characters.\n- For a reference implementation, see Karpathy's minbpe (three tiers: BasicTokenizer / RegexTokenizer / GPT4Tokenizer).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAny byte sequence can be encoded, so there is no OOV, vocabulary size is controllable, and it is friendly to multilingual text and code. The cost is that a Chinese character initially takes 3 bytes, and if the vocabulary has few Chinese merges, one character needs 2–3 tokens. Naive training is O(num_merges × N); HF tokenizers and tiktoken use a priority queue + linked list for incremental updates. A larger vocabulary means shorter sequences but larger embedding / lm_head parameters (Llama 3 went from 32k to 128k; GPT-4o's o200k is about 200k).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: Chinese, emoji, and the empty string all round-trip losslessly.\n- Difference from WordPiece: WordPiece picks pairs by likelihood gain; SentencePiece treats spaces as ordinary characters.\n- Optimization: deduplicate and count by pre-tokenized \"words\", and incrementally update only the affected pairs.","L7-7.2.4":"When BPE encodes new text, it must replay merges **in the training-time merge order (rank)** rather than recounting frequencies; combined with regex pre-tokenization and special-token handling, this yields segmentation consistent with training.\n\n**How it works**\n- Encode: loop over all adjacent pairs in the current ids, merge the pair with the smallest rank in merges (learned earliest), until no mergeable pair remains. Later merges often depend on tokens produced by earlier ones, and merging out of order yields segmentation inconsistent with the training distribution.\n- Regex pre-tokenization (GPT-2/4 style): first split text into \"word / number / punctuation / whitespace\" chunks and merge only within chunks, preventing `\"dog.\"` and `\"dog!\"` from each becoming a single token. GPT-4's regex splits numbers into groups of at most 3 digits, which helps with arithmetic.\n- Special tokens: recognized as a single id only when `allow_special=True`; otherwise they are tokenized as ordinary text.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIf special tokens can be injected through user text, they break the conversation structure, which is a security issue; the default should be `allowed_special=\"none\"`. Pre-tokenization prevents cross-word merges but also limits compression; SuperBPE first learns subwords within whitespace boundaries, then lifts pre-tokenization to learn superwords spanning whitespace, cutting token counts by up to 33% at a 200k vocabulary.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Naive encoding is O(n²), but chunks are short so it's acceptable.\n- Assertion: in the regex version, every token contains at most 1 leading space.","L7-7.3.1":"LoRA freezes the pretrained weights W and trains only a pair of low-rank matrices to represent the weight update. It is the most widely used parameter-efficient fine-tuning method, and hand-writing a LoRA linear layer is a basic question for fine-tuning roles.\n\n**How it works**\n- Forward: `y = xW + (xA)B·α/r`; A maps down to rank r, B maps back to the output dimension, and `α/r` is the scaling factor.\n- Initialization: A uses Kaiming uniform, **B is set to zero**, ensuring the initial ΔW = 0 so the model starts from its pretrained state. If both A and B were 0, the gradients would always be 0 and it would never learn.\n- Merging: `W' = W + AB·α/r`; since linear operations are associative, it is merged into W at deployment, with zero extra inference overhead.\n- Trainable parameters drop from `d_in·d_out` to `r(d_in + d_out)`; rsLoRA changes the scaling to `α/√r`, which is more stable at large r.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n`α/r` means you don't need to retune the learning rate when changing r. LoRA's lr is usually an order of magnitude larger than full fine-tuning (rule of thumb 1e-4–2e-4 vs 1e-5), because the update is scaled, there are few parameters, and B starts from 0. When serving multiple adapters simultaneously, don't merge; keep the base shared and select the adapter per request. QLoRA's base is 4-bit, and merging then re-quantizing introduces error, so it is usually merged into a bf16 copy.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: initial output == base; outputs are identical before and after merge / unmerge.\n- Be able to derive `dB = s·(xA)ᵀ·dy` and `dA = s·xᵀ·(dy·Bᵀ)` by hand and check them against numerical gradients.","L7-7.3.2":"AdamW is the default optimizer for LLM training. The key to hand-writing a single step is bias correction and **decoupled weight decay**, which is exactly what distinguishes it from Adam + L2.\n\n**How it works**\n- `m = β1·m + (1-β1)·g`, `v = β2·v + (1-β2)·g²`.\n- Bias correction: `m̂ = m/(1-β1ᵗ)`, `v̂ = v/(1-β2ᵗ)`. m and v start at 0, and without correction the first few steps would be too small.\n- Update: `p ← p·(1 - lr·wd) - lr·m̂/(√v̂ + ε)`. The decay shrinks p directly and does not add `wd·p` to the gradient.\n- At step 1, `m̂ = g` and `√v̂ = |g|`, so the update is about `lr·sign(g)`, equivalent to sign-SGD.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAdam + L2 adds `λθ` to the gradient, where it gets adaptively scaled by `1/√v̂`, so parameters with large gradients actually decay less, making regularization strength uneven. Memory-wise, each parameter has two fp32 states, m and v; together with fp32 master weights and bf16 weights and gradients, mixed precision needs about 16 bytes/parameter, which is exactly what ZeRO shards. Muon applies Newton–Schulz orthogonalization to the momentum of 2D weights, while embeddings, norms, etc. still use AdamW.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertion: with zero gradient, p is scaled by `(1 - lr·wd)`.\n- Common bugs: multiplying wd into m/v; inconsistent counting of `step` from 0 vs 1.","L7-7.3.3":"The learning rate schedule determines how lr changes during training. Warmup + cosine is the most common combination; WSD (warmup-stable-decay) is increasingly popular in pretraining.\n\n**How it works**\n- Warmup: over the first several steps, lr rises linearly from 0 to the peak, letting Adam's second-moment estimates stabilize first.\n- Cosine: afterwards `lr = min + ½(max - min)(1 + cos(π·progress))`, smoothly decaying to `min_lr` (often 10% of the peak).\n- WSD: after warmup, hold the peak during a plateau and anneal quickly only at the end; the benefit is that you can branch off from the plateau at any time to continue pretraining, without fixing the total number of steps in advance.\n- Assertions: warmup is monotonically increasing; the end of warmup equals the peak, the midpoint equals the average of the two, and the end equals min_lr.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nYou must clamp beyond the total number of steps: the cosine formula makes lr rise again when progress > 1, while linear decay (e.g., the end of WSD) drops below min_lr or even goes negative. Cosine is simple and stable but requires fixing the total steps up front; WSD is more flexible and suits continued pretraining and checkpoint branching.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When writing a schedule function, proactively handle `step > total` and the convention of whether `step` starts at 0 or 1.\n- Be able to explain why warmup is needed: early second-moment estimates are inaccurate, and a large lr easily diverges.","L7-7.3.4":"DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) trains the policy directly on preference pairs without a separate reward model or PPO, and is the most commonly tested loss for offline alignment.\n\n**How it works**\n- `L = -log σ(β[(log π_c - log π_ref_c) - (log π_r - log π_ref_r)])`, where c is chosen and r is rejected. The implicit reward is `β·log(π/π_ref)`, and the loss is a logistic loss on the difference between two implicit rewards.\n- The sequence log-prob is the **sum** of response-token log-probs (masking out the prompt and padding), because sequence probability comes from the autoregressive factorization; averaging changes the meaning of β, which is SimPO's length-normalization design.\n- When policy == ref, the bracket is 0 and `L = -log σ(0) = ln2 ≈ 0.693`; the loss at the first training step should be exactly this value, a good sanity check.\n- Use a stable `log_sigmoid`; the ref model runs under `no_grad`, and ref logps can be precomputed offline.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDPO is offline, simple, and cheap, but limited by the preference data distribution; online RL can explore new answers and suits tasks with verifiable rewards. Larger β stays closer to ref, commonly 0.1; too small a β overfits the preference data and makes outputs longer.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Chosen probability also drops: DPO only constrains the difference, so when the two are similar, pushing down rejected also pushes down chosen; adding an SFT loss term helps.\n- Monitor reward accuracy and margin during training.","L7-7.3.5":"GRPO replaces value-model advantage estimation with within-group relative rewards and updates the policy with PPO's clipped loss. It has been the mainstream approach for RL training of reasoning models since 2025, and hand-writing its advantages and loss has become a frequent question.\n\n**How it works**\n- Advantage: sample G answers for the same prompt, `A_i = (r_i - mean)/(std + ε)`, and every token of an answer shares `A_i`; groups that are all right or all wrong have zero advantage, so guard against nan.\n- PPO clip: `ratio = exp(logp_new - logp_old)`, `L = -mean(min(ratio·A, clip(ratio, 1-ε_low, 1+ε_high)·A))`. With positive advantage, the gradient is 0 once ratio exceeds the upper bound; with negative advantage, it is 0 once ratio falls below the lower bound.\n- Dr. GRPO: points out that std normalization amplifies difficulty bias and length normalization makes wrong answers grow longer, so it uses `A_i = r_i - mean` and aggregates by a fixed constant.\n- DAPO: clip-higher raises the upper bound to 0.28 (lower bound 0.2), giving low-probability tokens more room to rise and preventing entropy collapse; it also uses token-level aggregation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDropping the critic saves about half the memory, at the cost of higher variance and needing G samples per prompt. Sample-level aggregation averages within each sequence first, so each token of a long answer gets less weight; token-level aggregation averages over all tokens together, so length no longer dilutes the signal.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertion: with ratio=1.3 and ε=0.2, the loss is -1.2, and increasing ratio further leaves the loss unchanged.\n- GSPO changes the importance ratio to the sequence level (length-normalized) and clips at the sequence level.","L7-7.3.6":"InfoNCE is the standard contrastive learning loss for training embedding / retrieval models: it makes a query more similar to its positive than to the other samples in the batch.\n\n**How it works**\n- First L2-normalize q and p, then `logits = q̂·p̂ᵀ / τ`, giving an N×N similarity matrix.\n- The labels are the diagonal, equivalent to an N-way cross-entropy: the diagonal entries are positives and the other N−1 items in the batch are negatives.\n- A symmetric version is common (averaging q→p and p→q), which is what CLIP does.\n- Assertions: with perfect matches the loss ≈ 0; at random initialization the loss ≈ `ln N`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA small temperature τ gives a sharper distribution that focuses more on hard negatives but trains less stably; a large τ is smoother with weaker discrimination; 0.01–0.1 is common. Without L2 normalization, temperature loses its effect. The main problems with in-batch negatives: first, **false negatives**, where another item in the batch is actually relevant; second, the number of negatives is limited by batch size and memory. Remedies include deduplication, hard negative mining, gathering negatives across devices, and GradCache.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Mine hard negatives from BM25 or the previous model's top-k, but filter out \"false hard negatives\" that are actually relevant using a cross-encoder.\n- When domain performance is poor, first build a labeled query–doc set to measure recall@k, then fine-tune with domain data + hard negatives, or add a reranker.","L7-7.4.1":"BM25 is the classic lexical retrieval scoring function, and in RAG it still complements vector retrieval: for proper nouns, code identifiers, IDs, and rare entities, lexical matching is more reliable than dense vectors.\n\n**How it works**\n- `score(q,d) = Σ_t IDF(t) · tf·(k1+1) / (tf + k1·(1 - b + b·dl/avgdl))`.\n- IDF uses the Lucene form `ln(1 + (N - df + 0.5)/(df + 0.5))`, which is always positive; the original Robertson formula goes negative when df > N/2.\n- Three parts: IDF rewards rare terms; the term-frequency component saturates, approaching `k1+1` as tf grows instead of growing linearly as in TF-IDF; length normalization keeps long documents from winning just by having more words.\n- Implementation: an inverted index `term → [(doc, tf)]`; a query only visits documents containing the query terms, with complexity proportional to the total length of the posting lists.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nk1 controls how fast term frequency saturates (commonly 1.2–2.0), and b controls the strength of length normalization (commonly 0.75): b=1 is full normalization, b=0 none. BM25 for Chinese requires word segmentation (jieba, etc.) or character / bigram splitting, and segmentation quality directly affects recall, which is one motivation for learned sparse methods such as SPLADE.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: common terms have lower idf than rare ones; at equal tf, shorter documents score higher; results match hand calculations.\n- In production it is commonly combined with vector retrieval in a hybrid setup, fused with RRF.","L7-7.4.2":"These three are common small components in a retrieval pipeline: cosine top-k handles dense recall, RRF fuses results from multiple retrievers, and MMR removes redundancy from results.\n\n**How it works**\n- Cosine top-k: normalize vectors first, then take dot products; use `argpartition` to get k items and sort only those k, for O(N·d + N + k log k), cheaper than a full sort. Forgetting to normalize turns it into a dot product, favoring long documents.\n- RRF: `score(d) = Σ_i 1/(k + rank_i(d))`, with rank starting at 1; k=60 is the empirical value from the original paper. A document ranked first in both lists scores 2/61. The larger k, the flatter the differences between ranks.\n- MMR: greedily select one at a time `argmax λ·sim(q,d) - (1-λ)·max_{s∈S} sim(d,s)`. λ=1 reduces to pure relevance, and smaller λ means more diversity; incrementally maintaining \"max similarity to the selected set\" brings it down to O(k·N).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBM25 and vector scores are on different scales; RRF uses only ranks, needs no normalization, is robust to outlier scores, and works with zero tuning when there is no labeled data; with labeled data, learned weighted fusion or a reranker has a higher ceiling. In RAG you can also hard-deduplicate by source / document id, which is cheaper and more interpretable than MMR.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Starting RRF ranks at 0 is a common bug that miscomputes the weight of the top result.\n- MMR assertion: with two exact-duplicate candidates, both are selected at λ=1, and the second is skipped at λ=0.5.","L7-7.4.3":"Fixed-window chunking with overlap is the most basic chunking method in RAG. The code is only a few lines; what interviewers look at is boundary handling.\n\n**How it works**\n- Window size size, stride `stride = size - overlap`, requiring `0 ≤ overlap < size`; when overlap ≥ size, the stride is ≤ 0 and the loop never ends.\n- Each chunk records its `start / end` offsets in the original text, for citation tracing and highlighting.\n- The last chunk has `end = min(start + size, len)`, and stop once the end is reached, avoiding a redundant tail chunk consisting only of overlap; short text produces just one chunk.\n- Sentence-aware version: split into sentences on sentence-ending punctuation, then greedily pack, with adjacent chunks overlapping by one sentence; a single overlong sentence is hard-split at max_chars. Chinese sentence endings (。！？) are not followed by a space, so the sentence-splitting regex must not require whitespace after punctuation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOverlap prevents an answer from being cut exactly at a boundary, at the cost of duplicated content in adjacent chunks, so after reranking, deduplicate by document or merge adjacent chunks. A common starting point is 256–512 tokens with 10%–20% overlap, followed by a grid search on a retrieval eval set. In production, measure length in tokenizer tokens, prefer splitting along Markdown headings and paragraphs, and keep tables and code blocks whole.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: the overlapping parts of adjacent chunks are identical; concatenating after removing the overlaps reconstructs the original text losslessly.\n- Advanced approach: parent-child, retrieving small chunks and returning the parent chunk on a hit.","L7-7.4.4":"A header-aware chunker splits by Markdown heading structure and prepends the heading path (breadcrumb) to each chunk. It is the minimal implementation of structure-aware chunking and a live coding question for RAG roles.\n\n**How it works**\n- Scan line by line, representing the current heading path with a stack of `(level, title)`.\n- On a heading line: first flush the current buffer, then pop stack entries whose level is ≥ the new heading's (pop on same or higher-level headings), then push the new heading.\n- Lines inside code fences (three backticks) are not headings: maintain an `in_fence` toggle; `# comment` inside a fence is a common source of bugs.\n- On flush, join the path as `A > B > C` and prepend it to the body; when it exceeds `max_chars`, split further on blank lines. Complexity is linear.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nHeading boundaries are usually semantic boundaries, and the breadcrumb gives isolated paragraphs the context of \"which chapter and section this belongs to\", helping both retrieval and citation; the cost is dependence on document structure and parsing quality, and it doesn't work on long text without headings, which must fall back to paragraph or sentence splitting.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Measuring length in tokens: swap the length function for the tokenizer's len, and fall back to sentence splitting when a single paragraph still exceeds the limit.\n- Treat tables and lists as unsplittable units; when over the limit, make them their own chunk and replicate the table header.\n- Test five kinds of cases: nested headings, pseudo-headings inside fences, empty sections, skipped heading levels (# straight to ###), and an overlong single paragraph.","L7-7.4.5":"IVF and HNSW are the two mainstream families of approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexes. IVF is suitable for hand-coding; HNSW usually only needs to be explained verbally.\n\n**How it works**\n- IVF: train nlist centroids with k-means and put each vector into the inverted list of its nearest centroid; at query time, compute distances to all centroids first, then brute-force search for top-k only within the nearest nprobe inverted lists. Query complexity is O(nlist·d + nprobe·N/nlist·d). Larger nprobe gives higher recall and higher latency; with `nprobe = nlist` it degenerates to exact search with recall 1.\n- HNSW: a multi-layer proximity graph, like a skip list, where sparse upper layers enable long-range jumps and the dense bottom layer ensures recall. On insertion, greedily descend from the top layer, running a beam search with `ef_construction` at each layer and choosing M neighbors; at query time, `ef_search` controls accuracy vs latency.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nHNSW has low latency and high recall but uses a lot of memory (roughly vectors + M·N edges), builds slowly, and handles frequent deletions poorly (only tombstones). IVF uses little memory and builds quickly, and with PQ compression suits billion-scale data and GPUs. Tune with the recall–latency curve: empirically, IVF's nlist is on the order of √N.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertion: recall rises monotonically with nprobe.\n- Filtered search: post-filtering may return fewer than k results, while pre-filtering breaks the connectivity of the HNSW graph; you can build per-tenant indexes, or fall back to brute force when the filter keeps only a small fraction.\n- PQ: split vectors into m segments, each with a 256-centroid codebook, quantizing each segment to 1 byte.","L7-7.4.6":"recall@k, MRR, and nDCG@k are the three basic retrieval evaluation metrics, answering \"how much was found\", \"how high is the first correct result\", and \"how good is the overall ranking with graded relevance\" respectively.\n\n**How it works**\n- recall@k = `|top-k ∩ rel| / |rel|`, where the denominator is the total number of relevant documents; using k as the denominator makes it precision.\n- MRR = the mean of `1/rank(first relevant result)`, with rank starting at 1, suited to single-answer settings (QA, navigation).\n- nDCG@k: DCG applies a logarithmic position discount (e.g., `Σ rel_i / log2(i+1)`, or the graded version `(2^rel - 1)/log2(i+1)`), then divides by the IDCG of the ideal ranking; return 0 when IDCG is 0.\n- Edge cases: define whether to return 0 or skip when there are no relevant documents; k larger than the returned list length.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nrecall@k matters most for RAG, because the downstream LLM can only see what was retrieved; the retrieval stage often looks at @50 (the reranker's input), while what finally goes to the LLM is measured with precision@5 / nDCG@10. Good retrieval metrics don't guarantee good end-to-end results, since generation is also affected by lost in the middle, noisy documents, and chunk integrity. Confidence intervals on small eval sets are wide, so compare approaches with paired tests.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Assertions: a perfect ranking gives nDCG=1; moving relevant documents down lowers nDCG; MRR matches hand calculation.\n- Without human labels, generate synthetic questions with an LLM, or label with LLM-as-judge and then calibrate with human spot-checks.","L7-7.5.1":"The tool dispatcher (ToolRegistry) is the boundary between the agent and the outside world and must \"never crash\". Interviewers will probe item by item whether you meet the engineering requirements below.\n\n**How it works**\n- A registry `name → (schema, fn)`; flow: look up the tool (unknown → structured error with the list of available tools) → parse the argument JSON → JSON Schema validation (type / required / additionalProperties / enum / ranges), with errors carrying a path such as `$.city: expected string` → execute and catch all exceptions → truncate the result → uniformly return `{ok, content | error}`.\n- Errors are **fed back to the model** as tool results so it can self-correct in the next turn; only unrecoverable system errors terminate the loop.\n- `definitions()` outputs tools sorted stably by name, which helps prompt caching; the MCP 2026-07-28 spec also recommends that `tools/list` return a deterministic order.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLMs producing malformed arguments is normal, and calling directly with `**kwargs` raises TypeError on a single extra field. A hand-written validator covers only a subset; production should use a full JSON Schema 2020-12 implementation, or enable the vendor's strict mode (OpenAI requires `additionalProperties: false` and all fields required). You also need permissions, timeouts, idempotency keys, and audit logs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Test coverage: unknown tool, invalid JSON, missing required fields, type errors (`True` must not count as an integer), out-of-range values, extra fields, exceptions inside the tool.\n- Run parallel tool_calls concurrently with `asyncio.gather` and fill results back by `tool_call_id`.","L7-7.5.2":"The ReAct loop is the minimal skeleton of an agent: the model alternates between reasoning and calling tools until it gives a final answer. The key points of a hand-written version are that it **stops, is testable, and is debuggable**.\n\n**How it works**\n- Loop: `llm(messages) → tool_call | final`; a tool_call goes to the dispatcher and its result is appended with `role=tool`; a final answer is returned.\n- Abstract the LLM as an injectable function and use a scripted mock in tests: the first step deliberately passes a wrong argument name → receives a validation error → corrects it → two calculation steps → final answer 17, for 4 steps in total.\n- Layered termination backstops: final, `max_steps`, token / cost budget, repeated-action detection (same tool with the same arguments N times in a row). The infinite-loop mock stops after 3 steps via `max_steps`.\n- Return a `trace` (thought / action / observation / duration for each step) and a `stopped_reason`, distinguishing normal completion from being cut off by a limit.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFeeding tool errors back as observations gives the model a chance to correct itself, which saves tokens compared with blind retries, but the number of corrections should be capped. Don't pick `max_steps` arbitrarily: take the p99 step count of successful tasks on the eval set, leave about 1.5x headroom, and continuously monitor how often the limit is hit. The original ReAct wrote Thought/Action in text and parsed it with regexes; today APIs natively return structured tool_calls, but the loop structure is unchanged.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Context growth: summarize old observations, store large results externally and keep only references, hand subtasks to sub-agents.\n- Stuck or runaway cost: look at the `stopped_reason` distribution and p99 step count, and hash-count `(tool, args)` to find repeated actions.","L7-7.5.3":"A mini state graph executor mimics LangGraph's core mechanism: state flows between nodes, nodes return partial updates, and the executor handles merging, routing, and persistence.\n\n**How it works**\n- State is a dict, and a node maps `state → partial_update`; updates are merged with per-key reducers, overwriting by default, while `messages` uses `operator.add` to append—otherwise history is lost.\n- Edges are either normal or conditional: a router returns a name, optionally mapped via `path_map` to the next node or END.\n- `compile()` does static checks: there must be an entry point and every node must have an outgoing edge (dead-node detection); a production version should also check for unknown and unreachable nodes.\n- `invoke()` saves a checkpoint (node name + state snapshot) at every super-step and raises an error beyond `recursion_limit`. Since LangGraph 1.0.6, `recursion_limit` defaults to 1000 steps.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nReducers matter for parallel branches: when multiple nodes update the same key in one super-step, you need a merge rule, otherwise the later write overwrites the earlier one, so reducers should be mergeable. Checkpoints enable resuming from breakpoints, time-travel debugging, and human-in-the-loop, provided state is serializable and side-effecting nodes are idempotent. A common bug: mutating state objects in place, so checkpoints contaminate each other; copy with `dict(state)` at every step.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Human-in-the-loop: interrupt before a node, persist the state, and resume with the same thread id after human input.\n- Checkpoints can be stored in memory, SQLite, or Postgres.","L7-7.5.4":"Orchestrator-worker is the most common parallel multi-agent pattern: an orchestrator splits out subtasks, multiple workers call the LLM concurrently, and the results are aggregated at the end. When hand-writing it with asyncio, you must handle the concurrency cap, timeouts, partial failures, and result ordering.\n\n**How it works**\n- `asyncio.Semaphore(n)` caps the number of in-flight LLM calls, matching the vendor's concurrency and rate limits; the Semaphore must be inside the worker—placing it outside `gather` does nothing to throttle.\n- Wrap each call in `asyncio.wait_for(coro, timeout)`; a timeout cancels the underlying task. Timeouts usually aren't retried, while transient errors are retried with exponential backoff, using `await asyncio.sleep` for the backoff.\n- `gather(..., return_exceptions=True)`: one crashed worker doesn't cancel the others; sort results by task_id and explicitly include failures in the summary so the synthesizer knows information is missing.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n`TaskGroup` (3.11+) cancels the remaining tasks when any subtask fails, suiting all-or-nothing; here we need partial results, so use `gather`. Holding the Semaphore during backoff occupies a concurrency slot but puts less pressure on upstream. Estimate the concurrency cap with Little's law: in-flight ≈ QPS × average latency (5 QPS at 4 s needs about 20), then take the min with the vendor's limit. CPU-bound work should move to a process pool.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Tests: 6 subtasks with concurrency 3, asserting `max_in_flight ≤ 3`; inject three kinds of faults: succeeds after two failures, hangs forever, and exhausts retries.\n- Common bugs: `time.sleep` inside a coroutine, concatenating results in completion order.","L7-7.5.5":"Python 3.14 (released 2025-10-07) brought two changes directly relevant to concurrent agent code: asyncio task introspection tools, and free-threaded builds becoming officially supported (PEP 779).\n\n**How it works**\n- New `python -m asyncio ps PID` and `python -m asyncio pstree PID` print a running process's asyncio tasks and their await call chains; in code you can also get the call graph with `asyncio.capture_call_graph()`.\n- Debugging a stuck orchestrator: use `pstree` to see which await each task is stuck on (a tool call without a timeout, or a broken streaming response nobody handled), combined with each worker's `wait_for` timeout and span tracing to locate the problem.\n- Free-threaded builds remove the GIL, letting multiple threads truly execute Python code in parallel.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen an async program \"hangs\" there is no exception and no stack trace, making traditional debugging hard; visualizing await chains fills this gap. For GIL-related follow-ups, distinguish the scenarios: for I/O-bound work (LLM and API calls), asyncio is already enough; only CPU-bound work (local inference, parsing large files) needs a process pool or a free-threaded build.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"How do you debug a stuck orchestrator\": a timeout on every await + `pstree` to view await chains + spans on workers in production.\n- Don't treat free-threading as a replacement for asyncio; they solve different problems.","L7-7.6.1":"Exponential backoff retry is basic infrastructure for calling LLM APIs. Full jitter spreads out the retry times of many clients that failed simultaneously, avoiding a thundering herd.\n\n**How it works**\n- Before the n-th retry, wait `sleep ~ U(0, min(cap, base·2^n))`: the upper bound grows exponentially up to a cap, and the wait is sampled uniformly between 0 and that bound.\n- Retry only recoverable errors: 429, 5xx, connection resets, timeouts. Retrying 4xx errors such as bad arguments, auth failures, or content policy refusals is pointless; fail immediately, and re-raise the original exception after the final failure.\n- If the server provides `Retry-After`, honor it; also set an overall deadline and a retry budget (count / time / cost).\n- Make sleep and the random function injectable, and record the delay sequence in tests: with `max_attempts=8` there are 7 sleeps, all ≤ cap, and `ValueError` is not retried.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNon-idempotent operations (placing orders, sending email) must carry an idempotency key, otherwise retrying after a lost response executes them twice. LLM calls are semantically repeatable, but every call is billed and outputs differ. Retrying at every layer multiplies attempts (3 layers × 3 attempts = 27x amplification), so retry at only one layer, fail fast elsewhere, and add circuit breaking.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why jitter\" → to prevent all clients from retrying at the same instant and causing a thundering herd.\n- Retry storms: monitor the share of retried requests, and stop the bleeding with retry budgets and circuit breakers.\n- Use fault injection to compare success rate, p95 latency, and extra token cost with retries on vs off.","L7-7.6.2":"A token bucket handles rate limiting and LRU handles cache eviction; both are O(1) design questions, and in LLM services they correspond to managing vendor rate limits and caching results respectively.\n\n**How it works**\n- Token bucket: refill lazily `tokens = min(cap, tokens + Δt·rate)`; deduct if there are enough tokens, otherwise reject or compute `seconds_until`; no background thread is needed, and don't poll with sleep. Capacity determines the allowed burst, rate determines long-term throughput.\n- Inject the clock as a parameter so tests are deterministic: reject after a burst of 5, 2 tokens refilled after 1 second, and never above capacity even after a long idle period; `n > capacity` or `rate ≤ 0` should raise at construction or call time.\n- LRU: hash map + doubly linked list. In Python use `OrderedDict`: `move_to_end` on a hit, `popitem(last=False)` when over capacity; deleting from a list is O(n).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLM APIs usually have both RPM and TPM limits, so deduct by token count, not just request count. A token bucket allows bursts; a leaky bucket outputs at a constant rate; sliding-window counters are precise but storage grows with request count. In multi-instance deployments, centralized Redis + an atomic Lua script is globally precise but adds a network round trip; allocating local quotas per instance is simple but imprecise. On a 429, read `Retry-After` and feed it back to the local rate limiter.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Concurrency: use a lock for multithreading and `asyncio.Lock` for asyncio.\n- LRU's get also changes order, so reads need the lock too.","L7-7.6.3":"LLM streaming output uses SSE (Server-Sent Events). Network chunk boundaries have nothing to do with event boundaries, so the parser must be a stateful incremental parser rather than calling `split(\"\\n\\n\")` on the whole response.\n\n**How it works**\n- Maintain a byte buffer, cut complete lines on the line terminators `\\n`, `\\r\\n`, and `\\r`, and keep the remainder in the buffer; a lone `\\r` at the end must wait for the next chunk, since it may be a split `\\r\\n`.\n- A blank line ends an event; in `field: value`, strip only **one** space after the colon; lines starting with `:` are comments (often used as heartbeats); multiple `data` lines are joined with `\\n`; `event` defaults to `message`; the last event ID persists across events.\n- OpenAI chat.completions streams end with `data: [DONE]`; with `include_usage` enabled, the final chunk has empty `choices` and must be skipped.\n- Tool arguments: accumulate partial JSON fragments by content block `index`, and only after the block ends call `json.loads` once and run schema validation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen a JSON object is split across two chunks, non-incremental parsing breaks. The most effective test is to feed the same byte stream split into 1 / 3 / 7 / 1000-byte pieces and require identical results, which catches the vast majority of boundary bugs. If the UI needs to render tool arguments early, use a fault-tolerant partial JSON parser.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- TTFT = arrival time of the first content delta − request send time; `ping` and empty deltas don't count as the first token.\n- Handling dropped streams depends on the protocol: EventSource resumes with `Last-Event-ID`, while LLM APIs usually require re-issuing the request.","L7-7.6.4":"Streaming formats differ across vendors. The Anthropic Messages API frames SSE by content block, and you need to understand its event order to correctly assemble text, tool arguments, and thinking content.\n\n**How it works**\n- Event order: `message_start → (content_block_start → content_block_delta* → content_block_stop)* → message_delta → message_stop`, possibly interspersed with `ping` and `error` events.\n- Delta types: text increments are `text_delta`; tool-argument increments are `input_json_delta`, whose `partial_json` strings must be concatenated after `content_block_stop` and only then passed to `json.loads`; thinking content is `thinking_delta`, followed by a `signature_delta` before the block ends.\n- Compare OpenAI chat.completions: increments are in `choices[].delta`, and the stream ends with `data: [DONE]`. A practical `collect_text_deltas` can handle both formats.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNo individual fragment of tool arguments is valid JSON, so parsing fragment by fragment is bound to fail; aggregate by block `index` until the block ends, then do schema validation and dispatch. `ping` is a heartbeat and must not count as the first token in TTFT. In multi-turn tool use, thinking blocks must also be passed back verbatim as the vendor requires.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write out the event order from memory and say that `input_json_delta` is concatenated before parsing.\n- Mention that `error` events may appear mid-stream and must be handled.","L7-7.6.5":"Semantic caching uses the embedding similarity of a query to decide \"has a similar question been asked before\", and on a hit returns the old answer directly, saving LLM call cost and latency.\n\n**How it works**\n- Compute the cosine between `embed(query)` and cache keys; it's a hit if the max is ≥ the threshold; on a miss, call the LLM and write the result, with capacity eviction and TTL.\n- Don't pick the threshold arbitrarily: collect labeled query pairs of \"should hit / should not hit\", plot hit rate vs false-hit rate, and choose the operating point by the cost of a false hit.\n- Keys must include the model version, system prompt version, and user / tenant dimensions, otherwise user A's personalized answer may be returned to user B.\n- The hashed bag-of-words used for the demo uses `zlib.crc32`, not the built-in `hash()` (which is randomly salted per process, so results aren't reproducible).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFAQ-style traffic gets a sizable hit rate; the main risk is cross-answering questions that are semantically similar but factually different, e.g., \"Singapore's GDP in 2024\" vs \"in 2023\", whose embeddings are very similar but whose answers differ, so entity / number consistency checks are often added on top of semantic matching. It differs from prompt caching: the latter caches the KV for an identical prefix and the answer is still freshly generated, with no correctness risk; semantic caching returns the old answer directly, saving more but with risk.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- At scale, replace the linear scan with HNSW, and put an exact-match hash cache in front.\n- After launch, monitor hit rate and cost savings, sample hits for human evaluation of correctness, and keep a control group that bypasses the cache.","L7-7.6.6":"Rolling-summary conversation memory splits a long conversation into two parts: the last few turns are kept verbatim and earlier content is compressed into a summary, keeping context length bounded.\n\n**How it works**\n- Structure: `summary` + the last `keep_recent` turns verbatim; `build_context()` outputs \"system summary + recent turns\".\n- Trigger: when the estimated tokens exceed the budget and there are enough old turns, pass the oldest turns together with the old summary to the summarizer to produce a new summary.\n- Test with a deterministic mock summarizer, asserting that recent turns are preserved verbatim, early information is still in the summary, and context length has an upper bound.\n- Edge case: with `keep_recent=0`, `turns[:-0]` is an empty list, causing no compression or even an infinite loop, so slice with positive indices.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe real risk is **summary drift**: summaries of summaries gradually lose numbers, constraints, user preferences, and decisions already made. Key constraints should be explicitly extracted into structured memory (key-value / fact table) and injected every turn, rather than hoping the summary keeps them. Compression also conflicts with prompt caching, since every rewrite of the summary changes the prefix, so compress less often or in batches. Summarization calls should be asynchronous, falling back to truncation on failure.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The summary itself keeps growing: set a length cap and require rewrite-and-compress, or layer it into a session summary, topic summaries, and a long-term fact table.\n- Record the number of compressions and summary length to aid troubleshooting.","L7-7.7.1":"Algorithm questions for AI engineering roles are usually a notch easier than for pure backend roles, mostly LeetCode medium, but are often combined with the domain. By frequency they can be grouped into ten categories, each with a corresponding AI-scenario variant.\n\n**Checklist and variants**\n- Heaps / top-k (Kth largest, merge k sorted lists) → merging vector-search top-k results, merging multi-way retrieval.\n- Hashing / sliding window (two sum, longest substring without repeating characters) → n-gram counting, token-level deduplication, chunk sliding windows.\n- Two pointers / binary search → finding a threshold over sorted scores, binary-searching batch size.\n- Stacks / strings (valid parentheses, decode string) → parsing nested JSON / tool-call expressions.\n- BFS / DFS / topological sort (course schedule) → scheduling an agent's subtask DAG, cycle checking in a state graph.\n- Dynamic programming (edit distance, LCS, word break) → text alignment, WER, optimal segmentation.\n- Design questions (LRU / LFU, rate limiter, trie, union-find) → prefix matching for token-constrained decoding.\n- Concurrency (producer-consumer, blocking queue) → asyncio orchestrator, continuous batching scheduler.\n- Probability / sampling (reservoir sampling, weighted random) → data-mixture sampling, top-p.\n- Matrices / numerics (blocked matrix multiplication, k-means) → FlashAttention's tiling idea, IVF.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Mention the domain variant while answering to show you can transfer the idea.\n- Algorithm questions are the passing bar; engineering questions are what set candidates apart.","L7-7.7.2":"The core of preparing for coding-from-scratch questions is building a problem bank that is manageable in size yet comprehensive, and making \"write tests first\" muscle memory.\n\n**How it works**\n- Combine \"this chapter's scripts + 2–3 LeetCode problems per category\" into a bank of about 60 problems, and get each one to the point of writing it within 15 minutes, unprompted, with its own tests.\n- In the interview, **write tests before the implementation**; it's the cheapest way to earn points. Be able to write in both numpy and torch, since some companies forbid torch and others forbid numpy.\n- Practice narrating in English: for every line you write, say one sentence about a shape or invariant.\n- Systems-side questions (agent loop, retries, rate limiting) separate candidates more than model-side questions, because most candidates with a research background have never written production-grade error handling; this is the weak spot to focus on.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nToo large a bank can't be finished, too small won't cover enough; about 60 problems covers the ten frequent algorithm categories and also runs through the hand-written implementations on the model, retrieval, and agent sides. Timed practice should take two forms: pure hand-coding without AI, and AI assistant allowed with the focus on verification.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Timed mock: each session, draw 2 implementations—one in 30 minutes without AI, one in 20 minutes with AI allowed—narrating in English throughout and recording for review.\n- Acceptance criterion: by week 4, 80% of problems pass their assertions within 20 minutes without AI.","L7-7.7.3":"Implementing a queue with two stacks is a classic question; interviewers often follow up on thread safety and then extend to a thread-safe LRU, testing amortized analysis and \"atomicity of compound operations\".\n\n**How it works**\n- Enqueue only pushes onto the `in` stack; on dequeue, if `out` is empty, pour all of `in` into `out`, then pop. Each element is moved at most once, amortized O(1), and you can assert that the number of moves == the number of elements.\n- Thread safety: \"check empty → pour stacks → pop\" must happen under **the same lock**, otherwise two consumers may pour simultaneously or pop from an empty stack. The simplest correct solution is a single mutex used for both reads and writes. Splitting it into two locks offers limited benefit and is error-prone; when blocking semantics are needed, use `queue.Queue` or `Condition`.\n- LRU: hash map + a hand-written doubly linked list (sentinel head and tail nodes remove boundary checks); `get` also moves the node to the head, making it a write operation, so both reads and writes need the lock (`RLock`).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIf the lock is too coarse, first quantify lock contention, then shard by key hash into multiple independent LRUs. In asyncio's single-threaded event loop, a critical section without an `await` is naturally atomic; with an `await` inside, use `asyncio.Lock`. In production, Python should prefer `functools.lru_cache` or a mature library, and Redis for distributed setups.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Test: 4 producers and 1 consumer, asserting that each producer's elements still dequeue in FIFO order.\n- After an LRU stress test, check that \"linked list length == dict size ≤ capacity\".","L7-7.7.4":"Task dependency scheduling models tasks as a DAG and requires executing them by dependency and priority, supporting cascading cancellation, and detecting circular dependencies; detecting circular references in a spreadsheet is a variant of the same problem.\n\n**How it works**\n- Kahn's topological sort: build an in-degree table and a reverse adjacency list, push tasks with in-degree 0 into a min-heap, and pop by `(priority, id)` to keep it stable and reproducible. If fewer tasks are popped than the total, there is a cycle, so sorting and cycle detection happen together.\n- Reporting the specific cycle: all remaining nodes have in-degree > 0, so following remaining dependencies from any node must eventually loop back, finding it in O(V), e.g., `a -> c -> b -> a`.\n- Cascading cancellation: BFS along reverse edges from the cancelled task, cancelling all transitive downstream tasks; completed ones are not rolled back.\n- Spreadsheet: three-color DFS marking (white unvisited / gray on stack / black done); encountering a gray node means a cycle; use iterative DFS to avoid hitting the recursion depth limit on long dependency chains.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAgree on semantics for unregistered dependencies up front and raise an error directly, to avoid one place treating them as satisfied while another waits forever; raise exceptions on invalid state rather than using `assert` (`python -O` strips asserts). When adding tasks incrementally, you only need to check whether the new edge can lead from the dependency back to the task itself, without recomputing the whole graph.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Tests: self-loops, diamond dependencies (shared downstream but not a cycle), unrelated nodes outside a cycle, and cancellation affecting only downstream tasks.\n- After a cell changes, find the transitive downstream along reverse edges and recompute in topological order.","L7-7.7.5":"A scheduled task scheduler must support adding, cancelling, and running tasks at a specified time, with follow-ups on priority, periodic tasks, and scaling to multiple workers. It is an engineering question in interview reports for roles such as FDE.\n\n**How it works**\n- Use a min-heap ordered by `(run time, priority, sequence number)`; the sequence number keeps entries with the same time and priority stable and comparable.\n- Cancellation uses **lazy deletion**: put the ID into a cancelled set, O(1); cancelled entries are discarded only when they reach the top of the heap.\n- The main loop pops due tasks, runs them, and sleeps until the next task's time; the clock is injectable so tests are deterministic and fast.\n- Periodic tasks compute their next run time after executing and are pushed back onto the heap.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWith multiple workers, state must move to shared storage: workers claim tasks via atomic updates with a **lease**, and if a worker crashes, the lease expires and the task can be claimed again. Since delivery is at-least-once, execution must be idempotent, otherwise two workers may run the same task twice. At millions of tasks, tier by time buckets (time wheel) and shard, keeping only near-term tasks in the in-memory heap.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Two workers ran the same task\" → lease timeout + idempotency key.\n- Be clear about the cost of lazy deletion: cancelled entries linger in the heap, so rebuild periodically if needed.","L7-7.7.6":"An in-memory KV store with transactions (begin / commit / abort) supporting nested transactions is a \"complete small system\" question from OpenAI / xAI interview reports, testing choice of data structures and boundary semantics.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Undo log**: `begin` pushes an empty dict; before each `set` / `delete`, if the key hasn't been recorded at the current level yet, record its old value from before the first modification.\n- Distinguish \"key didn't exist\" from \"value is None\", using a sentinel object to mark non-existence.\n- `abort`: roll back using the current level's log. `commit`: if there's an outer transaction, merge the log into the outer level (keys already recorded keep the outer level's old value), so an outer abort can still undo the inner changes; an outermost commit simply discards the log.\n- Calling commit / abort with no active transaction should raise a clear exception.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCopy-on-write of the whole dict is also semantically correct, but each begin is O(data size); the undo log's overhead is proportional to the number of modifications. For persistence, append to a WAL (write the log before changing memory) and replay after a crash; this is the basic idea behind database transactions. Concurrent transactions need locks or MVCC; this question has single-threaded semantics, so just explain the trade-offs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Tests: an inner abort rolls back only the inner level; after an inner commit, an outer abort undoes everything; a newly created key disappears after rollback.\n- Organize small-system questions into modules, with a main demo, boundary tests, and a README.","L7-7.7.7":"A concurrent web crawler tests the combination of asyncio concurrency control, when to deduplicate, timeouts, and rate limiting; it is a frequent question in Anthropic SWE interview reports.\n\n**How it works**\n- Abstract fetching as an injected `fetch(url)`, and use a fake site represented as a dict in tests.\n- Level-by-level BFS: for each level, fetch all URLs concurrently with `gather`, cap concurrency with a `Semaphore`, and wrap each request in a `wait_for` timeout; failures are recorded as `None` without dragging down other pages.\n- Deduplicate by adding to the `seen` set **when a link is discovered**, not when it is fetched, to avoid the same URL being fetched by multiple coroutines.\n- URL normalization: `urljoin` to make paths absolute, `urldefrag` to drop `#fragment`, follow only same-domain http/https links, and abstract robots rules via an `allow` callback.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA concurrency cap is not politeness: real rate limiting needs a token bucket per domain, taking a token before fetching, and honoring robots.txt's crawl-delay. Also cap redirect hops to prevent redirect loops. At a billion pages, replace `seen` with a Bloom filter or a distributed KV store, persist the queue, and shard by domain.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Test coverage: cycles, duplicate links, relative paths, cross-domain links, hanging pages, depth limits, peak concurrency.\n- Stuck pages: set connect and read timeouts separately, cancel and log on timeout, and circuit-break per domain if needed.","L7-7.7.8":"In a find-the-bug round you get a piece of Transformer or agent code with 4–5 hidden bugs to fix under a time limit; in a refactoring round you get deeply nested code whose tests all pass. Both test systematic debugging and engineering method.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Reproduce before guessing**: write a minimal failing test (incremental vs full mismatch, loss not decreasing, agent infinite loop); once you have a deterministic repro, bisect: compare first-layer q/k/v, then post-RoPE values, then the attention output.\n- Transformer bug checklist: scaling by d_model instead of head_dim, wrong mask direction / alignment, softmax over the wrong dimension, view directly after transpose, RoPE positions starting from 0, caching k before rotation, re-initializing after weight tying.\n- Agent bugs: swallowed tool exceptions, no step limit, tool results not following their corresponding tool_use, shared mutable default arguments, `gather` missing `return_exceptions`, `time.sleep`.\n- Rerun tests after each fix, don't change several things at once, and talk through your hypotheses and how you ruled them out.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen there are no tests or reference implementation, use property tests: softmax rows sum to 1, changing the future doesn't affect the past, overfit a small batch. In a refactoring round (about 100–120 lines in OpenAI interview reports), first add or confirm tests, then refactor in small steps (extract functions, early returns, remove duplication, rename), keeping everything green at each step, and finally verify extensibility against a new requirement.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- For each bug, state the symptom and root cause before changing anything, and add a regression test after fixing it.","L7-7.7.9":"The camelCase-to-snake_case function itself is only a few lines; the follow-up is the real point: a large repo is full of the old style, so how do you migrate the whole codebase automatically and safely?\n\n**How it works**\n- Conversion: use regexes to split on \"lowercase→uppercase\" boundaries and acronym boundaries, e.g., `getHTTPResponseCode` → `get_http_response_code`, then lowercase everything.\n- Migration must not use regex replacement on text; parse into an AST or use the language server's rename, so strings, comments, and unrelated tokens aren't changed by mistake.\n- Exclude external contracts: public APIs, serialized field names, schema references, overridden third-party methods; detect naming conflicts after conversion.\n- Execute in small batches by module, running tests and type checks for each batch, each as its own reviewable PR that can be easily rolled back.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis question separates \"can write a regex\" from \"understands the risks of large-scale code changes\". Deciding which names are public requires looking at export declarations, serialization and schema references, and usage in downstream repos. An LLM can propose candidate renames, but the changes must be executed by deterministic tools and verified by tests.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Proactively bring up test cases for acronym boundaries (consecutive capitals, digits).\n- Explaining batching, verification, and rollback earns more points than the code itself.","L7-7.7.10":"A few algorithm questions recur in interview reports from ByteDance, Taotian, and others: maximum subarray sum (returning the interval), the largest number below n built from given digits, edit distance, and linked list cycles.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Kadane**: `cur = max(a[i], cur + a[i])`; when `cur` goes negative, discard it and restart from the current element, tracking the start index, and update `(sum, left, right)` whenever it beats the global best. With all-negative input, return the largest element, and be explicit about tie rules. O(n) time, O(1) space. Circular array: total sum minus the minimum subarray, handling the all-negative case.\n- **Largest number below n** (n=23121, A={2,4,9}, answer 22999): find the longest prefix matching n digit by digit, at some position choose \"the largest available digit smaller than n's digit there\", and fill the rest with A's largest digit; if no prefix works, fall back to the largest number with fewer digits, and the leading digit can't be 0.\n- **Edit distance**: `dp[i][j] = min(delete, insert, replace)`, with a rolling array reducing space to O(min(m,n)).\n- **Linked list cycle**: fast and slow pointers, O(1) space.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGreedy questions have many edge cases (leading zeros, fewer digits, A having only one digit); a brute-force enumeration takes 5 lines, and cross-checking against it on thousands of random inputs is the cheapest guarantee of correctness. ACM-style format also requires handling input and output yourself, defining `ListNode` yourself, and constructing linked lists with cycles.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why Kadane is correct: a negative prefix can only make later sums smaller.\n- Follow the same process: signature and tests first, then implementation, and finally state the complexity.","L7-7.7.11":"Quick questions on Python fundamentals often come at the start or in the first round, using a few concepts to check whether you've really written production Python: when to use tuples, generators, decorators, and asyncio.\n\n**Key points**\n- **list vs tuple**: lists are mutable; tuples are immutable, and when all their elements are hashable the tuple itself is hashable, so it can be a dict key or set element, suiting fixed records.\n- **generator**: produces values lazily, streaming large files without loading them all into memory at once; compared with a list, memory use is constant rather than linear.\n- **decorator**: wraps cross-cutting logic around a function without changing its body, such as retries, timing, and audit logging. Use `functools.wraps` to preserve the original function's name, docstring, and signature information, otherwise debugging, logging, and framework reflection all break.\n- **asyncio**: overlaps the execution of I/O-bound LLM and API calls; unsuitable for CPU-bound tasks.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe GIL is a common follow-up: multithreading can't truly run in parallel for CPU-bound work, and asyncio is single-threaded cooperative concurrency, so both suit only I/O-bound work; CPU-bound work needs a process pool (or Python 3.14's free-threaded build). When answering experience questions, give concrete numbers for years, scale, and production deployment.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Follow-up chain: how the GIL affects asyncio and multithreading → memory difference between generators and lists → how decorators preserve signatures.\n- Examples land best when tied to LLM scenarios: wrapping retries with a decorator, streaming a corpus with a generator.","L7-7.7.12":"Given an event stream, return the k most frequent elements. The value of this question lies in comparing the complexity of two solutions, and in approximate approaches for unbounded streams.\n\n**How it works**\n- First count with a hash map, O(n).\n- **Heap**: maintain a min-heap of size k (or `heapq.nlargest`), O(m log k), where m is the number of distinct elements. Very cheap when k is much smaller than m.\n- **Buckets**: create `n+1` buckets indexed by frequency, scan from high to low frequency, and return once k are collected, O(n + m), linear.\n- **Unbounded stream, limited memory**: when exact counts don't fit, use a Count-Min Sketch plus a heap, or the Misra-Gries algorithm for approximate heavy hitters.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe heap approach is general and suits small k and data inserted incrementally; the bucket approach is faster when the frequency upper bound is known (≤ n) but needs n+1 buckets. Approximate algorithms trade a controllable error for constant memory, suiting real streams like logs and clickstreams. In AI scenarios, this maps to n-gram counting, identifying hot queries, and merging top-k from multi-way retrieval.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State the complexity before writing; proactively bring up edge cases such as tie ordering, empty input, and k larger than the number of distinct elements.\n- A common add-on is merging overlapping intervals (sort, then scan, O(n log n)).","L7-7.7.13":"Longest substring without repeating characters is the representative sliding-window question; the key to the O(n) solution is how the left end jumps, plus one condition that's easy to miss.\n\n**How it works**\n- Maintain the window's left end `start` and a hash map `last`: character → index of its most recent occurrence.\n- When visiting character ch at index i: if `ch in last and last[ch] >= start`, it repeats within the current window, so jump `start` to `last[ch] + 1`.\n- Update `last[ch] = i` and `best = max(best, i - start + 1)`.\n- Time O(n), space O(alphabet size).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n`last[ch] >= start` is a common bug: if omitted, an old index outside the left of the window pulls `start` backward, making the result too large. Recording the most recent index in a hash map lets the left end jump directly; shrinking the left end one step at a time with a set is also O(n) but with a larger constant. In AI scenarios this pattern corresponds to token-level deduplication and chunk sliding windows.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Test edge cases: empty string, all identical characters, all distinct characters, a repeated character occurring outside the window (e.g., `abba`).\n- It is often followed in the same session by implementing k-means in NumPy and its failure modes: dependence on initialization (use k-means++), convergence only to local optima, and handling empty clusters.","L7-7.7.14":"Some companies have started piloting coding rounds that allow an AI assistant. The focus then shifts from \"can you write it\" to \"can you decompose it, verify it, and catch the AI's mistakes\".\n\n**How it works**\n- First clarify requirements, design the interface, and write test cases yourself, then have the AI generate one specific function (e.g., line splitting for an SSE parser); don't throw the entire problem at the AI.\n- Read the AI's code line by line and run the tests, focusing on mistakes AI commonly makes: softmax without subtracting the max, an unhandled trailing `\\r`, blocking calls inside asyncio, swallowed exceptions.\n- Spend the time the AI saves on adding tests, error handling, complexity analysis, and discussing productionization (monitoring, rate limiting, cost).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMedia have reported that Meta has piloted AI-enabled coding rounds since 2025-10 (secondhand source; defer to the recruiter's instructions for details), and OpenAI interview reports have also described a format that allows AI but requires screen sharing and explanation. But most companies still keep hand-written rounds without AI, so practice the two formats separately.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"The AI's code passed the tests; do you trust it?\" → No. Tests only cover the cases you thought of; also review complexity, edge cases, and concurrency safety, and add property tests that cross-check on random inputs where needed.\n- You can connect this to how you evaluate the quality of AI-generated code in your day-to-day work.","L7-7.7.15":"Coding-from-scratch questions themselves change slowly, but after you write the basic version, interviewers often ask \"what do the latest models or recipes change here\". The 2025–2026 developments directly relevant to hand-written implementations are concentrated in RL losses, attention structure, protocol specs, and interview formats.\n\n**Main developments**\n- **RL loss**: DAPO (2025-03) with clip-higher (ε_low=0.2, ε_high=0.28) and token-level loss; Dr. GRPO (2025-03) removing std normalization of advantages and length normalization; GSPO (2025-07) making the importance ratio sequence-level with sequence-level clipping, used in Qwen3.\n- **Attention**: Gated Attention multiplies a per-head sigmoid gate after the SDPA output and before `o_proj`, mitigating attention sinks; it won a NeurIPS 2025 Best Paper award and was adopted by Qwen3-Next; DeepSeek-V3.2's DSA uses a lightning indexer to select top-k past tokens for sparse attention.\n- **Protocol**: the MCP 2026-07-28 spec becomes stateless, `inputSchema` allows any JSON Schema 2020-12 keyword, and `tools/list` should return results in a deterministic order.\n- **Interview format**: AI-assisted coding rounds are on the rise, shifting the focus to verification.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nConnecting a basic implementation to recent developments turns an ordinary hand-coding question into an in-depth discussion; for example, after writing MHA, add one line `out = out * sigmoid(x @ W_gate)` and explain that the gate gives the model an outlet to \"output nothing\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Being able to write GSPO's `ratio = exp(mean_t(logp_new - logp_old))` earns extra points.","L7-7.7.16":"The five trends in coding-from-scratch questions can be cited directly in interviews, showing that you have a view on how the topics are evolving rather than just memorizing problems.\n\n**Five trends**\n1. **The standard answer on the model side is changing**: RMSNorm + SwiGLU + RoPE + GQA is now the default combination, and follow-ups extend to gated attention, sparse attention, and MLA.\n2. **RL losses are the new frequent question**: since 2025 the GRPO family has replaced PPO as the mainstream for training reasoning models, and post-training roles almost always ask you to write GRPO advantages or the clipped loss.\n3. **Coding rounds for agent roles have become engineering-focused**: ReAct loops, tool schemas, concurrency, retries, and streaming parsing test the robustness of production code, not algorithmic tricks.\n4. **Library APIs are changing**: after Hugging Face Transformers v5 (2026-01), old idioms such as `load_in_4bit` are outdated, and quantization goes through `quantization_config=`; write code according to the official release notes, not from memory of two years ago.\n5. **AI-assisted interviews are rising**: the focus of evaluation shifts from \"can you write it\" to \"can you verify it\"; pairing every implementation with assertion tests handles both interview formats at once.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese five map to different preparation priorities by role: model / training roles lean toward 1 and 2, agent / application roles toward 3 and 5, and every role should watch 4.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how have coding questions changed recently\", organize your answer around these five, each with a concrete example.","L7-7.7.17":"The companion code is organized by topic into 9 runnable Python scripts (Transformer core, sampling and BPE, training components, retrieval, agent core, async multi-agent, infrastructure, supplementary interview questions, SQL) plus a small coupon system, and the hands-on exercises revolve around them, turning concepts into code you can write on the spot and prove correct.\n\n**Main exercises**\n- **Cross-check numpy ↔ torch**: run the PyTorch snippets in Colab, with max logits error < 1e-5 in fp32, and the KV cache consistency test also passing in the torch version.\n- **Load GPT-2 weights**: note that GPT-2 uses LayerNorm, GELU(tanh), learned absolute positional embeddings, and biases, and HF's `Conv1D` weights need transposing; logits error versus transformers < 1e-3, and 50 greedily generated tokens identical one by one.\n- **Productionize BPE**: train on 5–10 MB of mixed Chinese and English text, with per-word deduplicated counting and incremental pair updates; the merge table matches the naive version, with a speedup of ≥ 10x.\n- **Small retrieval benchmark**: run BM25, dense, RRF, and MMR on BEIR SciFact, cross-validating metrics with `ranx` or `pytrec_eval`.\n- **Fault-injection agent**: randomly inject 429 / 500 / timeouts / invalid arguments / dropped streams; at a 20% fault rate, 100 consecutive tasks run with no uncaught exceptions, and each task has a trace and a `stopped_reason`.\n- **Timed mocks**: 4 weeks, twice a week, one problem in 30 minutes without AI and one in 20 minutes with AI, narrating in English while coding and recording.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCross-checking and cross-validation turn \"I think it's right\" into \"provably right\"; the fault-injection agent can also go straight onto your resume as project experience.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to cite concrete experiences such as \"I verified the KV cache with incremental vs full assertions\".","L7-7.8.1":"For an open-ended hand-coding question like a \"high-concurrency coupon distribution system\", the first step isn't writing code but spending about 2 minutes clearing up ambiguities in the requirements. The prompt only says \"different user segments get different offers, high concurrency, consider edge cases\", and each fuzzy point maps to a design decision; if you write without asking, you'll end up starting over.\n\n**How it works (five key clarifications)**\n- Overlapping segments (VIP and at risk of churning): issue only one offer according to a unique priority, and \"no stacking\" is a campaign-level rule—the first successful claim locks in the coupon template, after which switching keys, explicitly specifying another segment, or a VIP tier upgrade all return `LIMIT_REACHED`. Preventing stacking only within a single request isn't enough: switch the key and specify a different segment, and you can claim again.\n- Per-user limits and idempotency are managed separately: `per_user_limit` handles \"clicking twice\", while the idempotency key handles \"timeout retries of the same request\"; the idempotency store caches only terminal states, not results that may change later such as `NOT_STARTED` or `SOLD_OUT`.\n- For \"high concurrency\", build single-process thread safety first; put the multi-instance design in the README without implementing it.\n- The campaign claim window `[valid_from, valid_until)` and the coupon's own `expires_at` are two different things.\n- Claiming several coupons at once with insufficient stock: all or nothing.\n\n**Why it matters**: Clarification is itself a scoring point, showing requirements-analysis ability; it also narrows the scope to something deliverable in 75 minutes.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Proactively distinguish two kinds of duplicates: retries with the same key vs repeated clicks with a new key.\n- Explain why \"not started\" isn't cached: otherwise a key used before the start would get \"not started\" forever after the campaign begins.\n- Explicitly state what you won't build (multi-instance) and how you'd extend to it later.","L7-7.8.2":"\"Interface first\" means settling the external interface and return type before writing any implementation: `claim(user, idempotency_key, quantity, segment) -> ClaimResult`, plus a status enum (e.g., success, `NOT_ELIGIBLE`, `NOT_STARTED`, `SOLD_OUT`, `LIMIT_REACHED`, etc.). From the signature alone, the interviewer immediately understands what you're building and where the boundaries are.\n\n**How it works**\n- The whole service exposes a single `claim` entry point; `quantity` and `segment` are keyword arguments, and `segment` is optional (e.g., a VIP-only campaign page explicitly specifies the segment).\n- Separate exceptions from business outcomes: invalid input (quantity of 0, negative, `bool`, `float`; a nonexistent segment name raises `UnknownSegmentError`) raises an exception directly without changing any state, similar to HTTP 400; business outcomes such as sold out, expired, or not started are expressed as a status code in the return value, similar to 200 + a status code.\n- The status enum has a `retryable` attribute that decides whether a result can be written to the idempotency cache; replayed results are marked `replayed=True`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Settling the contract first lets the strategy classes, inventory, and tests all build around the same interface, and test cases can cover the status enum value by value. Making business outcomes return values rather than exceptions makes handling clearer for callers and avoids using exceptions for normal control flow.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If asked \"why not use exceptions for everything\": distinguish caller errors from normal business outcomes.\n- The same idempotency key with different arguments should raise an error (parameter fingerprint mismatch), not return the old result.\n- Invalid input must not consume the idempotency key or produce side effects.","L7-7.8.3":"The prompt requires that \"modules use an inheritance-based architecture\", and the key is to apply inheritance where things actually vary: segment matching (who gets it) and the offer (what they get). The main claim flow is fixed and written in the service as a template method, and each segment is a subclass of the `DistributionStrategy` abstract base class.\n\n**How it works**\n- The base class declares `name` and `priority`, an abstract method `matches(user, now)` to decide segment membership, and `template_for(user)` returning the coupon template, which subclasses can override.\n- Four subclasses: `WinBackStrategy` (has past orders and inactive for ≥ 30 days, priority 40), `VipStrategy` (`vip_level >= 1`, tiered by the highest tier reached: silver coupon for levels 1–2, gold coupon for ≥ 3, priority 30), `NewUserStrategy` (registered ≤ 7 days with no orders, priority 20), and `GeneralStrategy` (fallback, priority 0).\n- `StrategyRegistry` rejects duplicate names and duplicate priorities, and `resolve` picks the highest-priority segment among all matches, so \"VIP + churning\" is always `win_back` and \"new user + VIP\" is always `vip`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: A new segment = one new subclass + one registration line, following the open-closed principle without touching the main flow. The anti-pattern is creating a class for every field, which is over-engineering. Unique priorities make overlap resolution deterministic, avoiding implicit rules such as depending on registration order.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why priorities must be unique: otherwise results are nondeterministic when segments overlap.\n- When a segment is specified explicitly, return `NOT_ELIGIBLE` if the user doesn't qualify, and raise an exception if the segment doesn't exist.\n- Be able to say this is a combination of the strategy pattern and the template method.","L7-7.8.4":"The root cause of over-issuing coupons is the check-then-act race: `if remaining >= q: remaining -= q` is two steps, and multiple threads can pass the check at the same time and then all deduct. The fix is to make \"check + deduct\" a single atomic step, and also to complete the same user's idempotency check, offer locking, limit check, and bookkeeping within one critical section.\n\n**How it works**\n- Stock: `StockLedger.try_reserve(template_id, qty)` holds one lock per coupon template; inside the lock it checks the remainder, returns False if insufficient, and deducts if sufficient; hot coupons don't block each other.\n- User: pick a sharded lock by `hash(user_id) % 64` so requests from the same user are serialized; inside the lock, first check the idempotency cache, then `_decide`: segment → not started / ended (half-open interval) → campaign-level offer lock → template-level per-user limit → deduct stock → issue the coupon (on failure, restore the stock, catching `BaseException`) → record and lock the offer.\n- Lock order is fixed as \"user shard lock → template lock\", so there is no deadlock.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: A single global lock is also correct, but it serializes all users, giving single-threaded throughput; sharded locks keep the number of locks bounded while letting different users mostly avoid blocking each other, with contention only for \"the same user\" and \"the single line of code for the same template\". Python's GIL doesn't let you skip locks: the GIL only guarantees atomicity of single bytecodes, and since 3.13 there are free-threaded builds (PEP 703), which became officially supported in 3.14 (PEP 779).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Name the two races: check-then-deduct on stock, and the same user clicking concurrently with different keys.\n- Why fix the lock order: to avoid deadlock.\n- Mutation check: replace `try_reserve` with an unlocked version that inserts `sleep(0.001)` in the middle, and the concurrency tests fail immediately.","L7-7.8.5":"A complete hand-coded system question requires \"complete boundary tests\", and scoring looks at two things: whether boundary cases are fully covered, and whether the concurrency tests actually exercise the locks rather than passing by luck.\n\n**How it works**\n- Boundaries: stock of 0 (valid) and 1; a retry with the same key returns the same result marked `replayed`; repeated clicks with a new key are blocked by `LIMIT_REACHED`; the time window uses a half-open interval and is tested at `±1µs`; invalid quantities (0, negative, `True`, `float`) raise exceptions without side effects; duration parameters must be positive; a registration time later than now doesn't count as a new user; a failed issuance (including `KeyboardInterrupt` / `CancelledError`) must also restore stock.\n- Concurrency: use `threading.Barrier` to start 64 threads simultaneously competing for a template with stock 1, asserting exactly 1 success and remaining stock 0; concurrent attempts with different keys or different segments can also succeed only once.\n- Injectable clock: the service accepts a `now` provider, and tests set the time directly without `sleep`, keeping tests fast and stable.\n- Mutation check: deliberately change stock deduction to an unlocked \"check then deduct\" with `sleep(0.001)` inserted, and both concurrency tests fail immediately.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Without a Barrier, threads start at widely varying times, the race is hard to reproduce, and tests pass as false positives. At the end, the demo prints the invariants (no overselling, nobody over the limit, consistent retries) as an end-to-end self-check.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How to prove the concurrency tests are effective: mutation testing.\n- Why not use `sleep` in tests: slow and flaky; use an injectable clock instead.\n- Also cover cases with overlapping segments.","L7-7.8.6":"The single-process coupon system relies on in-process locks for correctness; once deployed as multiple instances, in-process locks no longer work, and atomicity and uniqueness must be pushed down into shared storage. In the interview, this goes into the README's \"production scaling\" section, marked as not implemented.\n\n**How it works**\n- Stock: keep it in Redis and use a Lua script to atomically run \"if `GET >= qty` then `DECRBY`\" (or `DECR` first, and `INCR` back if it goes negative).\n- Limits and idempotency: unique constraints on the coupon table `(user_id, template_id, seq)` and `(user_id, idempotency_key)` as the backstop, with `SET key NX EX` up front for fast deduplication; a unique constraint `(campaign_id, user_id)` on the offer-lock table.\n- Hot keys: at hundreds of thousands of QPS on a single template, split stock into N sub-keys, route requests to a random bucket, and try other buckets when one is empty; combine with gateway token-bucket rate limiting and queued admission, and if needed pre-allocate quota locally and replenish periodically.\n- Consistency: treat the Redis deduction as the commit point, and persist coupon records asynchronously via MQ (Kafka / RocketMQ) with idempotent consumers; beyond the retry limit, messages go to a dead-letter queue, and a reconciliation job compares Redis counts with DB records and restores stock; alternatively, use an outbox / transactional messages so \"deduction\" and \"pending coupon record\" succeed together.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Redis carries the throughput and DB unique constraints guarantee eventual correctness; the two are eventually consistent rather than strongly consistent, so reconciliation is mandatory. Splitting into sub-keys means \"remaining stock\" must be summed across them, and one bucket can be empty while others still have stock.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What about 10 instances: Redis Lua + unique constraints as the backstop.\n- Redis deduction succeeds but the DB write fails: MQ retries + idempotent consumption + dead-letter reconciliation.\n- Monitoring: alert on stock below 0 and on coupon issuance latency.","L7-7.9.1":"Hand-coding rounds often include a SQL question, and job descriptions that explicitly require SQL include DBS, OpenAI FDE, and Micron SMAI. Candidates who have worked on Text-to-SQL are especially assumed to be fluent at writing it by hand, because evaluation requires reading model-generated SQL and judging whether the error is in a JOIN condition, GROUP BY granularity, NULL semantics, or the window frame.\n\n**Answering process**\n- First state \"what one row of the result represents\" (granularity) and the output columns.\n- Write step by step with CTEs, so each step can be verified on its own.\n- Hand-check with 3–5 sample rows.\n- Proactively say how NULLs, ties, and duplicate data are handled.\n\n**Dialect differences (frequently tested)**\n- Date arithmetic: SQLite `date(d, '+1 day')`; PostgreSQL `d + INTERVAL '1 day'`; MySQL `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 DAY)`.\n- Integer division: SQLite and PostgreSQL truncate (`1/3 = 0`); MySQL's `/` returns a decimal, and `DIV` is integer division.\n- Month truncation: `strftime('%Y-%m', d)` / `date_trunc('month', d)` / `DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m')`.\n- A DELETE subquery referencing its own table: MySQL raises error 1093 and needs an extra derived-table wrapper.\n- Ignore on conflict: `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` vs MySQL `INSERT IGNORE`.\n- Viewing execution plans: `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` / `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` / `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` (MySQL 8.0.18+).\n\n**Why it matters**: Get the granularity wrong and everything after it is wrong; ignore dialects and your whiteboard answer won't run on the interviewer's database.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Start by stating the granularity and output columns, then write the code.\n- For every question, proactively discuss at least one boundary around NULLs, ties, or duplicate data.\n- Be able to switch dialects when asked how to write it in MySQL.","L7-7.9.2":"Per-group Top-N is the most frequent SQL question, e.g., \"the top 3 highest-paid employees in each department (including ties)\" or \"the second-highest salary in each department, returning NULL if there isn't one\". The core is choosing the right ranking function and wrapping the window function result before filtering.\n\n**How it works**\n- The three rankings on salaries 300, 200, 200, 150, 120: `ROW_NUMBER` gives 1-2-3-4-5; `RANK` gives 1-2-2-4-5 (ties take up slots, skipping numbers); `DENSE_RANK` gives 1-2-2-3-4 (no gaps).\n- \"The top 3 distinct salaries\" uses DENSE_RANK (returns 4 people); \"people ranked in the top 3, with ties taking slots\" uses RANK (returns 3 people); \"strictly 3 rows\" uses ROW_NUMBER and must include a tie-breaker (e.g., `id`).\n- Window functions are computed after `WHERE`, so they can't be used directly in `WHERE`; wrap them in a subquery or CTE and then `WHERE drk <= 3`.\n- Second highest: `GROUP BY dept` + `MAX(CASE WHEN drk = 2 THEN salary END)`, guaranteeing one row per department, NULL when there's no second place.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Using `LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1` directly returns an empty set rather than NULL when there's no second place, so wrap it in a scalar subquery; forgetting `DISTINCT` treats the two 200s as the second and third highest.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Without window functions (MySQL 5.7): a correlated subquery counting distinct salaries higher than one's own `< 3`, O(n²).\n- Large-table optimization: an index on `(dept, salary DESC)`; in PostgreSQL you can use `CROSS JOIN LATERAL (... LIMIT 3)`.\n- ROW_NUMBER is also commonly used for deduplication by \"taking the latest row per group\".","L7-7.9.3":"The same email appears multiple times in a signups table because of differences in case or leading/trailing spaces; the task is to find the duplicates, keep only the earliest row, and prevent recurrence. The key is first defining \"what counts as the same\", i.e., the normalization function `lower(trim(email))`.\n\n**How it works**\n- Find duplicates: `GROUP BY lower(trim(email)) HAVING COUNT(*) > 1`; `GROUP_CONCAT` doesn't guarantee concatenation order, so when a deterministic order is needed write `GROUP_CONCAT(id ORDER BY id)` or PostgreSQL's `string_agg(... ORDER BY id)`.\n- Deletion, approach one: `DELETE ... WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT MIN(id) ... GROUP BY lower(trim(email)))`.\n- Deletion, approach two: `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY lower(trim(email)) ORDER BY created_at, id)`, deleting `rn > 1`; to \"keep the latest\" instead, just change the ordering to DESC.\n- Prevent recurrence: an expression unique index `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON signups(lower(trim(email)))`, with writes using `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Doing `GROUP BY email` without normalization finds only some of the duplicates; cleaning up without adding a constraint means duplicates are back the next day. Confirm with a SELECT before deleting, and in production do it inside a transaction or write the ids to be deleted into a temp table.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- MySQL raises error 1093 when a DELETE subquery references its own table; wrap it in another derived table or delete via a self-join.\n- If the `NOT IN` subquery contains any NULL, the whole condition fails and nothing is deleted; use `NOT EXISTS` when unsure.\n- Hundreds of millions of rows: delete in batches by primary-key range, or build a new table via `INSERT ... SELECT` with deduplication and then swap; MySQL 8.0.13+ supports expression unique indexes via functional key parts.","L7-7.9.4":"Cohort retention groups users by their first login day and computes the share of each group that comes back on day 1 and day 7. It is the most common SQL question in data analysis and product evaluation.\n\n**How it works (three steps)**\n- Deduplicate to \"user × day\": `SELECT DISTINCT user_id, date(ts)`, so multiple logins in one day count once.\n- Each user's earliest day is their cohort: `MIN(d) GROUP BY user_id`.\n- Count with conditional aggregation: the denominator is the cohort size `COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)`, the numerator is `COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN a.d = cohort_day + N THEN user_id END)`; multiply by `1.0` when dividing, otherwise integer division in SQLite and PostgreSQL truncates to 0.\n- Immature cohorts (day N beyond the observation period) output NULL rather than 0%; in the example, the 7-day retention for 09-02 is NULL.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Definition errors are more common than syntax errors: \"day-7 retention\" (came back on that day) and \"7-day retention\" (came back on any day from 1 to 7) are two different metrics; also clarify up front whether cohorts are by signup date or first active date. Filling immature cohorts with 0 drags down the tail of the retention curve.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- A full 1–30 day retention matrix: first compute `day_n = date difference`, then `GROUP BY cohort_day, day_n`, and pivot in the frontend instead of writing 30 CASE expressions.\n- At large data volumes: first materialize a \"user × day\" activity table partitioned by day, and compute retention on the small table.\n- Proactively mention the two pitfalls of integer division and repeated logins.","L7-7.9.5":"The e-commerce funnel question asks for the number of people and conversion rate at each step in strict order (view → add to cart → pay). The difficulty is \"order\": people who added to cart before viewing, or who only paid, can't count toward later steps.\n\n**How it works**\n- Advance step by step with three CTEs: `s1` takes each user's earliest view time; `s2` joins `s1` with cart events on `e.ts > s1.t`, then takes `MIN(e.ts)`; `s3` similarly finds pay on top of `s2`.\n- Each step takes \"the earliest occurrence after the previous step's time\". This greedy choice is correct: an earlier anchor only gives later steps more candidates.\n- Finally `COUNT(*)` the rows of each CTE; each row is one user, so counting is naturally by person. The example yields 5 / 3 / 2, with step-to-step conversion of 60% and 66.7%, and 40% overall.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Ignoring order and directly using `COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event = ...)` gives 5 / 4 / 4, overstating the last two steps. Other common mistakes include counting events instead of people, counting each step independently so people in step two aren't in step one, and mixing conversion-rate denominators (step-to-step vs relative to step one).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Adding a \"completed within 24 hours\" window: add `e.ts <= s1.t + 24h`, but then greedily starting from the earliest view is no longer necessarily optimal; the strict solution must compute from every starting point, or use ClickHouse's `windowFunnel`.\n- A 10-step funnel: switch to time-ordered per-user event sequences and do subsequence matching in the application layer or a UDF.\n- State the definition of conversion rate up front.","L7-7.9.6":"Gaps-and-islands is the general technique for questions like \"logged in N consecutive days\", \"longest streak\", and \"session splitting\": cut an ordered series of records into contiguous segments (islands), then aggregate per segment to get start, end, and length.\n\n**How it works**\n- Step one must be deduplicating to \"user × day\".\n- Method A (date minus row number): `d - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d)`; within a run of consecutive dates, subtracting the increasing row number yields the same anchor date; then `GROUP BY user_id, anchor HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3`, with `MIN(d)` and `MAX(d)` as start and end. In PostgreSQL write `d - rn::int`, in MySQL `DATE_SUB(d, INTERVAL rn DAY)`.\n- Method B (general): use `LAG(d)` to compute the gap from the previous row, marking 1 (a new island starts) if it exceeds `max_gap` and 0 otherwise; a running `SUM(...) OVER (ORDER BY d ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)` over this flag gives the island ID. For each user's first row LAG is NULL, the comparison yields NULL, and it falls through to ELSE 1, correctly starting a new island.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Method A only handles \"strictly adjacent\" days; method B lets you tune `max_gap` (e.g., missing one day doesn't break the streak), and by replacing the day difference with \"time gap > 30 minutes\" it does sessionization. Skipping deduplication misaligns anchors: one island is split into two, or a spurious long island is stitched together.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Common mistakes: not deduplicating, differencing timestamps instead of dates, omitting `PARTITION BY user_id`.\n- Longest streak: wrap another `MAX(len)` around it.\n- Be able to explain why consecutive dates minus row numbers give the same value.","L7-7.9.7":"A self-join gives the same table two aliases and joins it like two tables, used for relationships between rows within a table, such as employees and their managers, or mutual follows between users.\n\n**How it works**\n- Paid more than their direct manager: `FROM employees e JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id WHERE e.salary > m.salary`. The join condition `e.manager_id = m.id` makes it explicit that m is e's manager.\n- Mutual-follow pairs: `follows f1 JOIN follows f2 ON f1.follower = f2.followee AND f1.followee = f2.follower`, then add `WHERE f1.follower < f1.followee` so each pair appears only once.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: The key is being clear about who is who in the join condition; swap the aliases and it becomes \"managers paid more than employees\". Without deduplicating mutual follows via `follower < followee`, each pair appears twice (in the example you'd get 4 rows instead of 2). To \"list every employee with their manager\", an inner join drops top-level employees without a manager, so use `LEFT JOIN`. A self-join only covers a fixed number of levels; hierarchies of arbitrary depth need a recursive CTE.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The full reporting chain (manager's manager, ...): a recursive CTE with cycle detection (`UNION` deduplication stops when it hits a cycle, while `UNION ALL` loops forever).\n- Mutual friends: self-join `follows` twice, then group by the pair and count; at large scale this is a graph query, so consider a graph database or offline computation.\n- How inner vs left joins affect the number of result rows.","L7-7.9.8":"Running totals and moving averages are both implemented with window aggregates `SUM/AVG(...) OVER (...)`, and the most error-prone part is the window frame: with `ORDER BY` but no frame specified, the default frame is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`.\n\n**How it works**\n- RANGE treats rows with the same sort value (peers) as a group: in the raw table, 09-01 has two records, and both rows get a running value of 42, so the running total appears to \"jump\"; switching to `ROWS` gives the two rows 42 and either 35 or 37 depending on processing order, a nondeterministic result.\n- The right approach: first `GROUP BY day` to aggregate to days, then apply the window, and both problems disappear.\n- Month-to-date total: `SUM(amt) OVER (PARTITION BY month ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)`; resetting by month requires `PARTITION BY`.\n- 3-day moving average: `ROWS 2 PRECEDING` means \"the previous two rows\", so when 09-03 is missing it pulls in 09-01 (8.333); for calendar days use RANGE with a date offset and divide by 3 (4.333). SQLite only supports numeric offsets, so window over `julianday`; PostgreSQL 11+ and MySQL 8 can write `RANGE INTERVAL` directly.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: ROWS takes the window by row count, RANGE by value; when dates have gaps, only RANGE gives \"the past N calendar days\". Using `AVG` for a moving average with missing dates divides by the actual number of rows, not 3 days.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Filling missing dates: a recursive CTE or a calendar table with `LEFT JOIN` + `COALESCE(amt, 0)`.\n- Year-over-year / period-over-period: `LAG(amt, 7)` or a self-join on dates.\n- Be able to state that the default frame is RANGE.","L7-7.9.9":"\"One user's orders for a given day are slow\" is a classic question on indexes and execution plans. The original `WHERE user_id = 44 AND date(created_at) = '2026-09-15'` wraps the column in a function; the index stores raw values, so the optimizer can't do a range lookup. Such a condition is called non-sargable.\n\n**How it works (four steps, checking the execution plan at each)**\n- Step 0: no index, `SCAN orders` plus a temporary B-tree for sorting.\n- Step 1: add only `INDEX(created_at)` with the query unchanged; it's still a full index scan.\n- Step 2: rewrite as a half-open interval `created_at >= '2026-09-15' AND created_at < '2026-09-16'` and create a composite index `(user_id, created_at)`; it becomes a `SEARCH`, and the sort is also satisfied by the index. The principle is equality columns first, range columns after.\n- Step 3: a covering index `(user_id, created_at, amount)`, so all queried columns are in the index with no table lookup, and drop the now-redundant step-2 index. Because `amount` now sits between `created_at` and the rowid, the tie-breaker `id` needs an extra sort, but only over the roughly 20 rows for that day.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**: Just saying \"add an index\" isn't enough; check whether the column is wrapped in a function and whether the composite index column order is right. Using `BETWEEN ... '23:59:59'` misses timestamps with milliseconds. Similar failures include `LIKE '%abc'`, implicit type conversion in MySQL, and `OR` across different columns. More indexes aren't always better: every index amplifies writes and takes memory.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Deep pagination with `OFFSET 100000`: switch to keyset pagination `WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_ts, :last_id)`.\n- If you can't change the SQL: create an expression index in PostgreSQL, or a functional index in MySQL 8.0.13+.\n- Always check the plan after adding an index: PostgreSQL `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`; in MySQL look at `type`, `key`, `rows`, and `Extra`."});
